                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "This game... where to begin.\n\nLet's start with the good points, I guess.\n\nPro:\nThe ball rolls about as you would expect, I guess?\nThe game actually runs very well for a UE4 title, probably because of its simplicity.\nSome of the level designs are interesting if you can endure the rather uninteresting and tedious early levels.\n\nCon:\n$4 pricetag for essentially an asset swap in what seems more like a hobbyist project than a commercially viable game. $1, perhaps, $4, no thanks. \nBlatant asset flip. You will be watching a generic blue/lightblue ball roll across the two generic sci-fi hex patterns that are featured in UE4 for the majority of the time. Most of the other assets remind me of generic space assets, probably freely available. While this isn't necessarily a bad thing, again, there is $4 pricetag. Considering that the achievement art looks like it was tossed together in paint and the menus are reminiscent of 2003, I'd say that this is not a particularly artistically endowed game. Again, you don't have to be a great artist, but a commercial game project typically doesn't just recycle art. The music is also generic and loopy.\nPhysics get wonky when two or more things colide at once, and surfaces behave unnaturally. The friction of the ball on the generic platform does not correspond to how it looks like it should. \nEarly levels are tedious and uninteresting.\nThe camera is kind of uncomfortable to adjust, and the menu does not lock the camera.\n\nWhile this game shows some okay principles underlying the gameplay, the actual design of the game is less satisfying and definitely does not justify the pricetag, especially since it seems more like a \"hey, look at what I can do with Unreal Engine 4\" than a finished product.",
